WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  security forces opened fire at crowds in  lagos and there are reports several  people have been killed  amnesty international says it happened  as they tried to disperse demonstrators  ahmed idris reports  just hours after a curfew came into  effect  the security forces started moving in  at the lekital gate in lagos several  different witnesses recorded footage of  what appeared to be  security personnel shooting at  protesters who were blocking a major  highway  a number of people were reported killed  it began as peaceful protest to demand  an end to police brutality  and for two weeks the government  struggled but failed to come  protesters their demands among others  include far-reaching reforms of the  police and the prosecution for officers  accused of extrajudicial killings  torture  and disappearances no care is going  through since we have been protesting we  have not had any reasonable results  so we want out buhari to come and  address us  if not possible  and address the citizens that's what we  can do  we have been trying we have been crying  every day so that they can hear  they can hear our voice but they don't  want to listen to us  but i believe this world are doing we  are going to  get good through good things  and senior lawmakers are lining up  behind some of the protesters demands  i will not sign off on a 2021 budget  that does not include adequate  provisions to compensate those who have  suffered violence  and brutality at the hands of the police  in nigeria in the last  decade which means the country's 2021  budget  could be delayed on monday protesters  blockaded murtara mohammed international  airport lagos  nigeria's busiest  the same day these images appeared of  prisoners among them violent criminals  escaping when two prisons in other  states  were attacked the prison authorities say  nearly 2 000 prisoners have escaped more  warring  is the looting of the armory in the  correction facility here in abuja  nearly two dozen cars were destroyed  already states have started enforcing  24-hour car views  among them the financial capital lagos  which is also home to the country's  busiest ports  this could mean more trouble for an  economy already in a poor shape  the president of the senate has asked  the president to address the nation to  calm the situation  but it's not clear if that will be  enough to placate the protesters  muhammad idris al-jazeera abuja

